const lessons:Record<string,Lesson[]> = {
money:[
{id:"money-1",title:"Welcome to your money story",minutes:8,objective:"Explain how a budget helps you make intentional choices with money.",body:["A budget is a plan for money you expect to receive and money you expect to spend. It is not a punishment or a promise to never have fun. It helps you decide what matters most.","Four building blocks are income (money coming in), expenses (money going out), savings (money set aside for a future purpose), and cash flow (income minus expenses over a period).","A useful budget is realistic, flexible, and updated when your circumstances change. Estimates are starting points—not guarantees. Keep planned amounts separate from what you actually spend."],example:"You receive $400 in a month. You plan $150 for savings, $120 for transport and phone costs, $80 for food, and $50 for flexible spending. The plan adds up to $400.",question:"What is the main purpose of a budget?",choices:["To stop all optional spending","To plan how money will be received, used, and saved","To guarantee there will never be an unexpected expense","To make every month exactly the same"],answer:1,explanation:"A budget is a flexible plan for income, spending, and saving. It cannot eliminate surprises, but it can help you prepare and make choices."},
{id:"money-2",title:"Income, expenses & cash flow",minutes:10,objective:"Distinguish income from expenses and calculate a simple monthly cash flow.",body:["Income is money you receive, such as pay from work or an allowance. For planning, use money you can actually spend—not money you hope to earn someday.","Expenses are costs you pay. Fixed expenses are usually similar each period, variable expenses can change, and irregular expenses happen occasionally. Setting aside a little for irregular costs can help.","Cash flow is income minus expenses. A positive result means more money came in than went out during that period. A negative result means expenses exceeded income and the plan needs attention."],example:"Monthly income is $520 and expenses total $445. Cash flow = $520 − $445 = $75 remaining.",question:"You receive $520 and spend $445 in a month. What is your cash flow?",choices:["−$75","$75","$445","$965"],answer:1,explanation:"Subtract expenses from income: $520 − $445 = $75. The result is positive, before any unlisted costs."},
{id:"money-3",title:"Build a starter budget",minutes:12,objective:"Create a balanced plan using categories and realistic estimates.",body:["Write down reliable income for the period, then list necessary expenses and their due dates. Include savings goals and occasional costs instead of hoping they will not happen.","Give yourself a realistic flexible-spending category. If a plan only works when nothing unexpected happens, it may be too tight. When income changes, adjust the plan.","Compare the plan with actual spending at the end of the period. Notice what surprised you and update the next plan. The goal is learning and control, not perfection."],example:"Fictional plan: income $600; bills $180; food and transport $160; savings $100; irregular-cost fund $60; flexible spending $100. Total planned uses = $600.",question:"What should you do if real expenses are consistently higher than your estimate?",choices:["Ignore the difference","Make the same estimate forever","Review actual costs and adjust the plan","Assume the extra cost will disappear"],answer:2,explanation:"Use real information to update your plan. A budget is most useful when it reflects what is actually happening."}],
banking:[{id:"banking-1",title:"What banks and credit unions do",minutes:8,objective:"Describe a deposit account and compare account terms.",body:["A deposit account can help you receive money, pay bills, and keep savings separate from everyday spending. Banks and credit unions may offer checking, savings, and other account types.","Compare monthly fees, minimum balances, ATM access, transfer options, and interest paid. A feature is only useful if it fits your needs and the account terms.","Check age requirements before opening an account. Never share passwords, one-time codes, or account details with someone who contacts you unexpectedly."],example:"Account A has no monthly fee but pays no interest. Account B pays some interest but charges a fee unless you keep a minimum balance. Compare full terms and your likely balance.",question:"What is a sensible way to compare two bank accounts?",choices:["Choose the most colorful advertisement","Compare fees, access, requirements, and interest together","Assume all accounts have identical terms","Share your login with someone who offers to help"],answer:1,explanation:"Compare the complete terms and how the account fits your circumstances. Protect your login and verification codes."},
{id:"banking-2",title:"Interest: what your balance earns",minutes:10,objective:"Calculate simple interest and explain why rates and time matter.",body:["Interest is money paid for the use of money. A savings account may pay interest, while borrowing usually means paying interest. The rate, balance, and time period all matter.","A simple-interest estimate uses principal × annual rate × time in years. Real accounts may compound interest, use changing balances, or apply specific account rules, so the simple formula is a learning model.","Compare the rate with account fees. A small amount of interest may not offset a fee, and a quoted rate is not a guarantee that every account term is favorable."],example:"A fictional $500 balance earns simple interest at 4% for one year: $500 × 0.04 × 1 = $20 before taxes or fees.",question:"Using simple interest, how much interest does $500 earn at 4% for one year?",choices:["$2","$20","$40","$504"],answer:1,explanation:"$500 × 0.04 × 1 = $20. Actual account calculations can differ based on compounding and terms."},
{id:"banking-3",title:"Build a savings goal",minutes:11,objective:"Turn a savings target into a manageable contribution plan.",body:["A useful savings goal names the target, amount, deadline, and reason. Break the remaining amount into weekly or monthly contributions that match the time available.","Keep near-term money accessible and consider the safety and protections offered by the account in your country. Investment products can lose value and are not the same as insured deposit accounts.","Review the plan if income or timing changes. A smaller sustainable contribution is more informative than a plan that depends on money you do not reliably receive."],example:"A fictional $180 goal with 12 weeks remaining requires $15 per week if you start from $0 and ignore interest.",question:"What makes a savings goal easier to follow?",choices:["A target with no deadline or amount","A plan based on hoped-for income","A clear target divided into realistic contributions","Assuming interest will cover the whole goal"],answer:2,explanation:"Specific targets and realistic contributions turn a goal into an actionable plan."},
{id:"banking-4",title:"Protect your accounts",minutes:9,objective:"Recognize common account-security risks and safer responses.",body:["Use unique passwords, multi-factor authentication where available, and account alerts. Never disclose a one-time login code to a caller or message sender who contacts you unexpectedly.","Fraudsters may pretend to be a bank, employer, friend, or government office. Urgency, secrecy, unusual payment methods, and requests for codes are warning signs.","If a message may be legitimate, contact the institution using the number or website you independently know is genuine. If you suspect unauthorized activity, contact the institution promptly."],example:"A text says your account will be frozen in ten minutes unless you send a verification code. Do not reply; contact the bank through its official app or known contact details.",question:"What is the safer response to an unexpected request for a one-time code?",choices:["Send it quickly to avoid a penalty","Post it publicly to ask if it is real","Do not share it; independently contact the institution","Forward it to another stranger for checking"],answer:2,explanation:"Verification codes protect account access. Do not share them with unexpected callers or messages."}
],
work:[{id:"work-1",title:"Gross pay and take-home pay",minutes:9,objective:"Explain the difference between gross pay and net pay.",body:["Gross pay is earnings before deductions. Net pay, often called take-home pay, is the amount left after deductions such as taxes or authorized contributions.","A pay stub may list hours, pay rate, gross earnings, deductions, year-to-date totals, and net pay. Review it and ask a trusted payroll contact about unfamiliar entries.","Rules differ by location and employment situation. For personal tax decisions, check official tax agencies or a qualified adviser."],example:"If gross pay is $300 and total deductions are $42, net pay is $300 − $42 = $258.",question:"Which amount is generally deposited after deductions?",choices:["Gross pay","Net pay","Hourly rate","Overtime rate"],answer:1,explanation:"Net pay is what remains after deductions. Gross pay is the amount before deductions."},
{id:"work-2",title:"Read a pay stub",minutes:10,objective:"Identify common pay-stub fields and spot questions worth checking.",body:["A pay stub may show the pay period, hours, rate, gross pay, each deduction, net pay, and year-to-date totals. Keep the pay period in mind when comparing two stubs.","Check whether hours and rates match your records and whether deductions are labeled. Some deductions are required by law; others may be benefits or authorized contributions. Rules vary by location.","If something seems wrong, save the stub and your hours record, then ask payroll or a trusted workplace contact for an explanation. Do not assume an unfamiliar line is automatically an error or automatically correct."],example:"A fictional stub shows 18 hours × $15 = $270 gross pay. If listed deductions total $31, estimated net pay is $239.",question:"What is a sensible first step when a pay-stub line is unclear?",choices:["Ignore it forever","Compare records and ask payroll for clarification","Assume all deductions are mistakes","Share the full stub publicly"],answer:1,explanation:"Check the records and ask an appropriate payroll contact. Keep personal and financial details private."},
{id:"work-3",title:"Taxes and withholding basics",minutes:11,objective:"Distinguish paycheck withholding from a final tax calculation.",body:["Withholding is money an employer sends toward certain taxes during the year. The final tax calculation may depend on total income, deductions, credits, location, and other rules.","Withholding is not always identical to the final amount owed. Depending on the rules and personal circumstances, a person may owe more or receive a refund after filing.","Tax rules change and differ by country and region. Use official tax-agency guidance for real decisions, and ask a qualified tax professional when a situation is complicated."],example:"A fictional worker has $40 withheld across several paychecks. That amount alone does not prove whether the worker will owe more or receive a refund at filing time.",question:"Does paycheck withholding always equal a person's final tax amount?",choices:["Yes, for every worker","No; the final result depends on the full tax situation and local rules","Only when paid weekly","Only when the pay stub is printed"],answer:1,explanation:"Withholding is a payment toward tax obligations; the final calculation depends on the complete situation and applicable rules."},
{id:"work-4",title:"Compare job offers thoughtfully",minutes:10,objective:"Compare pay with schedule, costs, safety, and written terms.",body:["A job offer is more than an hourly rate. Consider expected hours, schedule reliability, commute costs, training, benefits, duties, workplace safety, and whether the terms are provided in writing.","For variable or task-based pay, record completed work and the agreed payment terms. Ask how rates are set, when payment occurs, and who to contact about a discrepancy. Requirements differ by location and worker classification.","Do not share sensitive identity or banking details until you have independently verified the employer and understand why the information is needed. Be cautious if a supposed employer asks you to pay upfront to receive a job."],example:"Offer A pays a little more per hour but requires a costly long commute. Offer B pays slightly less but has predictable nearby shifts. Compare estimated take-home money and practical fit, not headline pay alone.",question:"Which is the strongest way to compare two job offers?",choices:["Choose only the largest advertised number","Compare pay, hours, costs, conditions, and written terms","Ignore the schedule and commute","Pay an upfront fee to prove you are serious"],answer:1,explanation:"A realistic comparison includes earnings and the practical costs and conditions of doing the job."}
],
credit:[{id:"credit-1",title:"Credit, borrowing & total cost",minutes:9,objective:"Identify why interest and fees affect the total cost of borrowing.",body:["Credit allows someone to borrow money or pay under an agreement with an obligation to repay. A credit card is not extra income; purchases generally create a balance that must be paid.","Interest is the cost of borrowing, often expressed as an annual percentage rate (APR). Fees and repayment timing can also affect total cost.","Understand repayment terms and whether payments fit your budget. Be cautious of pressure to act immediately. Credit rules vary by country."],example:"A loan with a low monthly payment may still cost more overall if the repayment term is much longer. Compare APR, fees, total repayment, and missed-payment consequences.",question:"What should you examine when comparing borrowing options?",choices:["Only the monthly payment","Only the brand name","APR, fees, repayment term, and total cost","Whether an offer expires in ten minutes"],answer:2,explanation:"The total cost and terms matter. High-pressure countdowns are a reason to slow down, not rush."},
{id:"credit-2",title:"Credit reports and scores",minutes:10,objective:"Explain what credit reports and scores can indicate and what they cannot.",body:["A credit report may contain information about borrowing and repayment history. A credit score is a number calculated using a particular scoring model and report data.","Reports can contain errors, and scoring models differ. A score is not a complete measure of a person's character or overall financial health. Access to reports and correction processes vary by country.","If you are eligible to access a report, use legitimate official or regulated channels, protect your identity, and dispute information you believe is inaccurate through the appropriate process."],example:"A report lists an account you do not recognize. Do not assume it is yours; review the report carefully and follow the legitimate dispute or fraud-reporting process for your location.",question:"What should you do if a credit report contains an account you do not recognize?",choices:["Ignore it without checking","Share your identity details with an unsolicited caller","Review it and use the legitimate dispute or fraud process","Pay anyone who promises an instant score increase"],answer:2,explanation:"Investigate unfamiliar information through official channels and protect personal information."},
{id:"credit-3",title:"Minimum payments and repayment",minutes:11,objective:"Explain why minimum payments may extend repayment time.",body:["A minimum payment is the smallest amount required by a credit agreement for a particular billing period. Paying only the minimum can leave a balance accruing interest and may make repayment take much longer.","The exact result depends on the balance, APR, fees, payment timing, and agreement terms. Paying late can also lead to fees or other consequences, depending on the contract and local law.","Before borrowing, understand the required payment and the total-cost estimate. If repayment becomes difficult, contact the lender through verified channels and explore legitimate options before ignoring the account."],example:"A fictional card balance of $600 at a high APR may remain for a long time if payments barely cover interest and fees. Use the actual agreement's terms to estimate repayment.",question:"Why can paying only the minimum be costly?",choices:["It always eliminates interest","The balance may shrink slowly while interest continues","It guarantees a higher credit score","It cancels the agreement"],answer:1,explanation:"When payments are small relative to interest and the balance, repayment can take longer and cost more."},
{id:"credit-4",title:"Borrowing alternatives and warning signs",minutes:10,objective:"Evaluate whether borrowing is necessary and recognize risky offers.",body:["Before borrowing, consider whether the purchase can wait, whether a lower-cost option exists, and whether the payment fits after essential costs. Avoid borrowing based only on a lender's maximum offer.","Understand APR, fees, due dates, total repayment, collateral, and what happens after a missed payment. A low advertised payment can hide a long term or extra charges.","Guaranteed approval, pressure to act immediately, requests for gift cards or cryptocurrency, and upfront fees to unlock a supposed loan are warning signs. Verify lenders through the appropriate official regulator for your location."],example:"An offer promises guaranteed cash today but demands a gift-card payment first. Do not pay; independently verify the company and seek advice from a trusted adult or appropriate consumer-protection agency.",question:"Which is a major warning sign in a loan offer?",choices:["Clear written terms you can review","Time to compare options","A demand for upfront gift-card payment","A transparent total-cost estimate"],answer:2,explanation:"Upfront gift-card payment demands and pressure tactics are common warning signs. Verify before sharing money or information."}
],
investing:[{id:"investing-1",title:"Risk, time & diversification",minutes:9,objective:"Explain why diversification can reduce concentration risk.",body:["Investing means putting money into assets with the hope of future growth, while accepting that values can fall. Returns are not guaranteed.","Diversification means spreading investments across different assets instead of depending on one company or asset. It can reduce the effect of a poor result in one holding, but cannot eliminate all risk.","Understand fees, time horizon, liquidity, and risk before making decisions. Be skeptical of promises of guaranteed high returns."],example:"If all invested money is tied to one company, a problem at that company could affect the entire amount. Holding different types of investments may reduce concentration risk, though markets can still decline.",question:"What is one purpose of diversification?",choices:["Guarantee profits","Remove every kind of risk","Reduce reliance on a single investment","Make fees irrelevant"],answer:2,explanation:"Diversification can reduce concentration risk, but it does not guarantee profit or prevent losses."},
{id:"investing-2",title:"Compound growth: assumptions matter",minutes:11,objective:"Describe how compounding works and why projections are not promises.",body:["Compounding occurs when earnings remain invested and future growth can apply to both the original amount and prior earnings. Regular contributions can also increase the amount invested over time.","A calculator projection depends on assumptions such as contribution timing, rate, fees, taxes, inflation, and volatility. A smooth assumed rate is a simplified model, not a prediction of actual market returns.","Compare scenarios rather than treating one number as certain. Money needed soon generally calls for different risk considerations than money intended for a distant goal."],example:"A calculator using a steady 5% rate will show a smooth path, but real investments can rise and fall. Label the result as an illustration with assumptions.",question:"How should you interpret a compound-growth calculator result?",choices:["As a guaranteed future balance","As a scenario based on stated assumptions","As proof that an investment cannot lose money","As a substitute for understanding fees"],answer:1,explanation:"A projection illustrates a hypothetical scenario; real results can differ substantially."},
{id:"investing-3",title:"Fees, inflation and real purchasing power",minutes:10,objective:"Explain how fees and inflation can affect long-term outcomes.",body:["Investment fees reduce the money that remains invested. Even small recurring costs can add up over long periods, so read fee disclosures and compare like with like.","Inflation means prices generally rise over time, so the same amount of money may buy less in the future. A balance can grow in dollars while its purchasing power grows more slowly.","Taxes, account rules, and fees depend on location and product. Use clear assumptions and avoid choosing a product based only on a headline return."],example:"If savings earn 3% in a year while prices rise 4%, the balance grows nominally but its purchasing power may decline before fees and taxes.",question:"What does inflation affect?",choices:["Only the color of a bank card","The purchasing power of money over time","Whether returns are guaranteed","Whether fees need to be checked"],answer:1,explanation:"Inflation can reduce what a given amount of money buys, even when the nominal balance rises."},
{id:"investing-4",title:"Investment accounts and time horizons",minutes:10,objective:"Connect the purpose of an account with access rules and time horizon.",body:["An account is the structure that holds assets; the investments inside it determine much of the risk and return. Account tax treatment, fees, contribution rules, and withdrawal rules vary by country.","A time horizon is when money may be needed. Short-term essential money generally should not depend on a volatile investment being up on a particular date.","Before opening an account, understand eligibility, fees, tax treatment, protections, and withdrawal conditions. Learn the rules for your country rather than assuming a product works the same everywhere."],example:"Money needed for a near-term rent deposit has a different job from money set aside for retirement decades away. The risk and access requirements are different.",question:"Why does a time horizon matter?",choices:["It changes when you need access and how much volatility may be manageable","It guarantees a return","It makes all accounts identical","It removes taxes and fees"],answer:0,explanation:"When money is needed affects how much risk and access uncertainty may be appropriate."}
],
household:[{id:"household-1",title:"The true cost of a home",minutes:10,objective:"Identify costs that belong in a realistic housing budget.",body:["Housing costs may include rent or a mortgage, utilities, internet, insurance, deposits, moving costs, and building or administrative fees. The exact categories depend on the location and contract.","Separate recurring monthly costs from one-time move-in costs. Ask what is included in advertised rent, confirm due dates, and keep a buffer for irregular expenses.","For international planning, record the original currency and conversion assumption. Exchange rates and transfer fees can change the final amount."],example:"A fictional apartment costs 2,300 PLN in rent, 500 PLN in building fees, and an estimated 250 PLN for utilities and internet. The estimated monthly total is 3,050 PLN.",question:"What is the best way to estimate monthly housing costs?",choices:["Use rent alone even if fees are separate","Add recurring costs and label estimates","Ignore utility costs","Convert currency without recording the rate"],answer:1,explanation:"A useful estimate includes the recurring costs you are responsible for and makes uncertainty visible."},
{id:"household-2",title:"Needs, wants and recurring bills",minutes:10,objective:"Prioritize essential household costs and identify flexible categories.",body:["Household budgets include recurring essentials such as housing, utilities, food, transport, and communication, plus irregular costs such as repairs, replacement items, and annual fees.","Needs and wants are useful planning categories, but context matters. A phone may be essential for work or safety; a flexible category can still support enjoyment and wellbeing.","Estimate costs using current local information, note what is included, and leave room for seasonal changes. Avoid assuming a listing or online estimate captures every fee."],example:"A fictional household has $1,500 available. List required bills first, then food and transport, savings or a buffer, and finally flexible spending. Check that all planned uses fit.",question:"What should a realistic household budget do?",choices:["Include only rent","Account for recurring essentials, irregular costs, and flexible spending","Assume every bill stays identical forever","Treat estimates as guaranteed prices"],answer:1,explanation:"A complete plan includes both predictable and irregular costs, while recognizing estimates can change."},
{id:"household-3",title:"Currency conversion and transfer costs",minutes:11,objective:"Convert currencies using an assumed rate and identify real-world differences.",body:["To convert from one currency to another, multiply by the stated exchange rate when the rate is quoted as target-currency units per one source-currency unit. Always label the direction of the rate.","Providers may use different rates and charge transfer or conversion fees. The rate shown in a search result may not be the rate applied when a transfer is completed.","Record the date, source, rate direction, and fees in an international budget. For a real transaction, check the provider's final quote before confirming."],example:"At a fictional rate of 1 USD = 4 PLN, $125 × 4 = 500 PLN before any fees or rate differences.",question:"At 1 USD = 4 PLN, what is $125 before fees?",choices:["31.25 PLN","125 PLN","500 PLN","4,125 PLN"],answer:2,explanation:"Multiply $125 by 4 PLN per USD to get 500 PLN before fees."},
{id:"household-4",title:"Build a relocation budget",minutes:12,objective:"Separate one-time relocation costs from recurring living costs.",body:["A relocation plan can include travel, document fees, deposits, first month's housing, temporary accommodation, essential setup items, and a contingency buffer. Some costs are paid once; others recur every month.","Check entry, residence, work, health-insurance, and document requirements through official sources for the destination and your circumstances. Never assume a tourist entry rule grants a right to live or work long-term.","Use conservative estimates and label unknowns. Plan for timing as well as totals: a deposit and first month's rent may be due before regular income begins."],example:"A fictional plan separates one-time travel and setup costs from monthly rent, food, transport, and internet. The initial cash needed can be much higher than one month's recurring budget.",question:"Why separate one-time and recurring relocation costs?",choices:["They are always the same amount","It shows both the initial cash needed and ongoing monthly needs","It eliminates uncertainty","It makes exchange rates irrelevant"],answer:1,explanation:"Initial setup costs and ongoing expenses affect different parts of a plan and should be tracked separately."}
],
data:[{id:"data-1",title:"Read a financial table",minutes:9,objective:"Check labels, units, totals, and missing information in a dataset.",body:["Financial data is useful only when you understand what each number represents. Check column names, time periods, currency, units, and whether values are planned or actual.","A total can look precise but be wrong if a row is missing or counted twice. Check arithmetic and look for unusual values before drawing conclusions.","When sharing findings, explain what the data supports and what it cannot tell you. Label fictional practice data, estimates, and verified figures."],example:"A table lists food $120, transport $45, and phone $35. Total listed spending is $200. Before concluding this is all spending, check for missing categories or dates.",question:"Before interpreting a financial table, what should you check?",choices:["Whether colors look attractive","Labels, units, period, and missing values","Whether every number is large","Whether the total confirms your guess"],answer:1,explanation:"Understand labels, units, dates, and completeness before drawing conclusions."},
{id:"data-2",title:"Spreadsheet formulas and validation",minutes:10,objective:"Use a total formula and a basic check to reduce spreadsheet errors.",body:["A spreadsheet can calculate totals, differences, percentages, and averages. Make sure the formula covers the intended cells and uses consistent units and periods.","Validation checks include comparing a formula with a hand calculation, checking that subtotals match the total, and confirming that values are not accidentally counted twice.","Keep raw data separate from calculated fields where possible, label formulas, and retain a copy of the original data. A spreadsheet can produce a neat result even when the inputs are wrong."],example:"If B2:B5 contain 45, 30, 25, and 10, their total is 110. A SUM formula should cover all four cells, and a hand check can confirm the result.",question:"What is a useful way to check a spreadsheet total?",choices:["Trust it because it is in a spreadsheet","Compare it with a hand calculation and check the cell range","Hide the formula","Remove the labels"],answer:1,explanation:"A second calculation and a range check can catch missing or duplicated values."},
{id:"data-3",title:"Choose the right chart",minutes:10,objective:"Match a chart type to the question being asked.",body:["Bar charts are useful for comparing categories, such as spending by category. Line charts are useful for showing change over time. A pie chart can show parts of a whole when categories are mutually exclusive and the total is meaningful.","Label axes, units, currency, and time periods. Start with a clear question and avoid visual choices that exaggerate small differences or hide important context.","A chart summarizes data but does not prove why a pattern happened. Describe the pattern, then state what additional information would be needed to explain causes."],example:"To compare food, transport, and phone spending in one month, a bar chart is usually clearer than a line chart. To show monthly spending over a year, a line chart may be useful.",question:"Which chart is usually best for comparing spending across categories in one month?",choices:["A bar chart","A line chart with unrelated dates","An unlabeled chart","A chart chosen only because it looks dramatic"],answer:0,explanation:"A bar chart makes category comparisons straightforward when labels and units are clear."},
{id:"data-4",title:"Write a careful data conclusion",minutes:11,objective:"Separate an observation supported by data from an unsupported causal claim.",body:["A strong conclusion states the observed pattern, includes relevant figures, and avoids claiming more than the data supports. Association does not by itself prove causation.","Mention important limits: small samples, missing records, estimates, unusual months, or categories that were not measured. Be transparent about how the data was collected and what time period it covers.","A useful report may include a next question to investigate. This turns a limitation into a plan for gathering better evidence rather than pretending uncertainty does not exist."],example:"If food spending rose from $100 to $130, you can say it rose by $30 in the recorded data. You cannot conclude why it rose without more information.",question:"What is a careful conclusion from spending data?",choices:["State a pattern the data supports and name important limits","Claim a cause without evidence","Ignore missing data","Treat estimates as verified facts"],answer:0,explanation:"Describe what the data shows, avoid unsupported causal claims, and state meaningful limitations."}
]
};
